title: The Biblical Doctrine of Reconciliation and Atonement
description: Reconciliation through Christ's blood: the atonement is the means, reconciliation is objective and cosmic in scope, and personal salvation is applied by faith in His risen life and kept secure.
output: lessons/heresies/reconciliation-and-atonement.html

# From the outline (studies/data/notes/reconciliation-and-atonement-notes.html), in the author's words. Every passage the
# outline quotes is pulled from the KJV by the builder; the author's bold words in them are kept as marks. Dashes and a
# full stop lost from the outline (double spaces) are restored.

=== Introduction
--- Reconciliation and Atonement [title]
overline: Answering error
heading: The Biblical Doctrine of Recon­ciliation and Atonement
sub: The Heart of the Gospel: Reconciliation Through Christ's Blood
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move through the slides. Tap any gold reference to read the verse.

--- Good tidings to all people
scripture: Luke 2:9-14 | mark: Glory to God in the highest, and on earth peace, good will toward men

=== I. The heart of the gospel
--- Reconciliation through Christ's blood
kicker: I. Introduction
lead: The Bible presents the cross as God's great plan to make peace and restore what sin broke.
p: **Atonement** (covering, satisfaction for sin) is the means God uses to accomplish **reconciliation** (restoring harmony, ending enmity).
p: Jesus’ death and blood is the **propitiation** (the price needed to satisfy God)
big: 1 John 2:2

--- Sufficient for all creation
kicker: I. Introduction
lead: Christ's death on the cross is sufficient and powerful enough to address the entire curse of sin on all creation. Yet personal salvation comes by faith in His resurrection, with eternal security for the believer.
scripture: 1 Timothy 2:5-6 | mark: for all

--- Two separate concepts
kicker: Key principle
lead: Reconciliation is objective and cosmic in its scope and accomplishment (through His death); personal salvation/justification is applied and secured through baptism into Christ, by faith in His risen life. These are two separate concepts.
callout: Without the atonement, justification would not be possible. But without justification of sinners, the atonement still has been paid.
big: Romans 5:10

=== II. The scope of reconciliation
--- All things reconciled to God
kicker: II. The Scope of Reconciliation
lead: Christ's blood makes peace for the whole creation disrupted by sin (the Fall affected everything: mankind, nature, heavenly powers).
scripture: Romans 5:12
scripture: Romans 8:22-23

--- Not a Universalist idea
kicker: II. The Scope of Reconciliation
- This is not a Universalist idea (the false doctrine that everyone is saved, with or without faith)
- Atonement/Reconciliation is **not synonymous** with personal salvation
- Despite reconciliation hell remains for the unsaved; and is reserved for the fallen angels.
- It is the triumphant, objective victory where all things are brought under Christ's lordship, where He has overcome the world.
- The cross subdues enmity, defeats powers, and prepares for the new heavens and new earth.

--- Peace through the blood of his cross
scripture: Colossians 1:20 | mark: to reconcile all things unto himself
scripture: Ephesians 1:10 | mark: gather together in one all things in Christ

--- Supporting cosmic impact
p: Creation groans under the curse but awaits deliverance ([[Romans 8:19-23]]); Christ triumphs over principalities and powers ([[Colossians 2:15]]).
scripture: Colossians 2:15

--- Not yet subjected
lead: All things are not yet subjected under Christ
scripture: Hebrews 2:8 | mark: But now we see not yet all things put under him
p: The total subjection purchased with the atonement, takes place in the End Times, after the resurrection, and His millennial reign.

--- That God may be all in all
p: And once Jesus, the Alpha and Omega, the LORD of Lords and King of kings has subjected all under Him, Jesus gives it to the Father:
big: 1 Corinthians 15:28

=== III. The means of reconciliation
--- By the death of God's Son
kicker: III. The Means of Reconciliation
- God Himself initiates and accomplishes reconciliation while we were still sinners and enemies—no human merit involved.
- The atonement is substitutionary: Christ died for sins, bearing the penalty, making peace through His shed blood.
- This objective work is finished—It is finished ([[John 19:30]])—sufficient to reconcile the world with God from its fallen state.

--- While we were yet sinners
scripture: Romans 5:8-10
scripture: 2 Corinthians 5:21 | mark: that

=== IV. The application to believers
--- Personal salvation by His life
kicker: IV. The Application to Believers
lead: While reconciliation is accomplished by His death (objective, for enemies), believers receive personal justification, peace with God, and eternal life through union with the risen Christ.
big: 1 Timothy 4:10 | mark: of all

--- Secure forever
kicker: IV. The Application to Believers
lead: Salvation is by grace through faith alone—once received, it is secure forever (OSAS), **because Christ ever lives to make intercession**, so believers are assured final salvation for those reconciled by faith.
scripture: Hebrews 7:25

--- Received the atonement
scripture: Romans 5:1; Romans 5:9-11 | mark: we have now received the atonement
note: atonement [reconciliation]
scripture: Romans 5:10 | mark: being reconciled, we shall be saved by his life

=== V. The ministry we have today
--- Proclaim the reconciliation
kicker: V. The Ministry We Have Today
- Believers are ambassadors with the message: Be ye reconciled to God!
- The gospel invites sinners to receive by faith what Christ has already accomplished.
scripture: 2 Corinthians 5:17-20 | mark: be ye reconciled to God

=== VI. Conclusion
--- The false dichotomy
kicker: VI. Conclusion
heading: The Biblical Doctrine of Atonement Destroys The False Dichotomy of Limited/Unlimited Atonement
lead: Christ's atonement is mighty: sufficient to reconcile all things through His blood (all creation’s victory over sin's curse), yet personally applied to save believers eternally by faith in His risen life.

--- Calvinism and Arminianism
kicker: VI. Conclusion
panel-warn: Calvinism | (Particular Redemption/Limited Atonement): Christ died efficiently and intentionally only for the elect (those unconditionally chosen by God before creation), securing salvation infallibly for them alone; His atonement is limited in scope and design, though sufficient in power for more if God had so decreed.
panel-warn: Arminianism | (Unlimited Atonement with Conditional Application): Christ died provisionally for all mankind without exception, making salvation genuinely available to every person; the atonement is unlimited in scope but limited in efficacy—applied only to those whom God foreknew would believe—thus election is conditional upon foreseen faith rather than sovereign decree alone.

--- For whom did Christ die?
kicker: VI. Conclusion
lead: Both Calvinism and Arminianism misunderstand the atonement by confining it primarily to the question of individual salvation ("for whom did Christ die?").
- **Calvinism** limits its scope and power to the arbitrarily elect.
- **Arminianism**, while claiming unlimited provision of the atonement, still ties its efficacy to foreseen individual faith, making election conditional rather than sovereign decree.
p: Both systems reduce the cross to an individualistic framework and miss its grand biblical purpose: the cosmic, triumphant reconciliation of all things—ending enmity, defeating powers, and restoring creation through the blood of the cross.

--- Two sides of a heretical coin
kicker: VI. Conclusion
lead: **Two sides of a heretical coin** - Arminianism essentially keeps the same Calvinistic framework (unconditional election based on God's decree) but swaps "arbitrary decree" for "foreseen faith"—so the atonement still ends up functionally limited in scope to the foreseen elect, just determined differently.
callout: Scripture presents a finished, objective victory already accomplished, rendering the debate moot.

--- Not willing that any should perish
scripture: 2 Peter 3:9 | mark: but that all
scripture: Hebrews 2:9 | mark: for every

=== Close
--- A finished, powerful work [title]
heading: A finished, powerful work
center: Scripture shows a finished, powerful work that glorifies God and is the power to secure the saved.
center: Christ died for all sinners, rose for our justification, and keeps us forever by the power of God.
big: John 3:16 | mark: the world
